      "output": "**Water** is a simple chemical compound that is essential for life on Earth. Here’s a concise overview of what water is, how it’s structured, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n## 1. Chemical Identity\n- **Formula:** **H₂O** – each molecule consists of two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom.\n- **Molecular weight:** ≈ 18.015 g/mol.\n- **Bond angle:** ≈ 104.5°, giving the molecule a bent (V‑shaped) geometry.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Physical States\n| State | Typical Conditions | Key Characteristics |\n|-------|--------------------|----------------------|\n| **Solid (Ice)** | ≤ 0 °C (32 °F) | Crystalline lattice, less dense than liquid water (hence ice floats). |\n| **Liquid** | 0 °C – 100 °C (32 °F – 212 °F) at 1 atm | Highly cohesive, excellent solvent, high surface tension, high specific heat capacity. |\n| **Gas (Water Vapor/Steam)** | ≥ 100 °C (212 °F) at 1 atm | Invisible gas; when condensed forms clouds, fog, or steam. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Unique Physical & Chemical Properties\n| Property | Why It’s Important |\n|----------|-------------------|\n| **Polarity** | The uneven charge distribution makes water a superb solvent for ionic and polar substances. |\n| **Hydrogen bonding** | Weak bonds between water molecules give rise to high boiling point, surface tension, and the anomalous density maximum at 4 °C. |\n| **High specific heat (4.18 J·g⁻¹·K⁻¹)** | Buffers temperature changes, stabilizing climates and biological systems. |\n| **High heat of vaporization (≈ 2260 J·g⁻¹)** | Drives evaporative cooling (e.g., sweating). |\n| **Cohesion & adhesion** | Enables capillary action, crucial for plant water transport. |\n| **Density anomaly** | Ice is ~9 % less dense than liquid water, allowing it to float and insulate aquatic life in winter. |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Role in the Environment & Life\n- **Universal solvent:** Dissolves salts, sugars, gases, and many organic compounds, facilitating biochemical reactions.\n- **Biological medium:** Constitutes ~60 % of the human body (by weight) and is vital for cellular processes, nutrient transport, and temperature regulation.\n- **Hydrologic cycle:** Evaporation → condensation → precipitation → runoff → infiltration → groundwater, continuously redistributing water across the planet.\n- **Climate regulator:** Oceans store and release heat, influencing weather patterns and global climate.\n- **Geological influence:** Water drives erosion, sediment transport, and rock weathering; it also participates in mineral formation (e.g., hydrothermal vents).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Everyday Examples\n- **Drinking water:** Provides essential hydration and minerals.\n- **Cooking:** Transfers heat efficiently; its boiling point is a reliable temperature reference.\n- **Cleaning:** Its polarity helps dissolve dirt, oils, and microbes.\n- **Industrial uses:** Coolants, solvents, steam turbines, and chemical synthesis.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Fun Facts & Trivia\n- **Three states in one:** In a “triple point” (0.01 °C, 611 Pa) water can coexist as solid, liquid, and gas.\n- **Surface tension:** A water droplet on a leaf forms a near‑perfect sphere because surface tension minimizes surface area.\n- **Electrical conductivity:** Pure water is a poor conductor; impurities (ions) dramatically increase conductivity.\n\n---\n\n### Bottom Line\nWater is a tiny, polar molecule (H₂O) whose ability to form hydrogen bonds gives it a suite of extraordinary physical and chemical properties. These properties make water the **“universal solvent,” a thermal buffer, a transport medium, and a life‑supporting substance**—fundamental to chemistry, biology, geology, and everyday human activity."
